  "query_preview": "..\nA trade-wind trough oriented from the northwest to the southeast will\nbring a brief wetter period of scattered showers and isolated\nthunderstorms tonight through Wednesday. Due to its orientation and \nnortheasterly motion, Saipan and Tinian will see these showers \nfirst, with the onset this evening for their coastal waters, and \nafter midnight for these islands. Remnant island-effect showers are \nongoing for southwestern Guam and adjacent coastal waters, so a \nthunderstorm or two should not be ruled out from now until shortly \nafter sunset. Then, isolated thunderstorms will become a concern for \nGuam and Rota after midnight, but the increase in shower coverage is \nnot expected until Wednesday morning. The duration of these showers \nheavily depends on the longevity of this feature, however, model \nguidance indicates a return to drier weather starting Wednesday \nafternoon for Saipan and Tinian, and Wednesday evening for Guam and \nRota. This along with slightly different wind directions out of the \nnorth and northeast supported a split zone, however, a return to one \nzone wouldn't be out of the question over these next few forecast \nissuances. Then, a potentially broad circulation may impact the \nregion around Thursday, so shower coverage tops out at 30 percent, \nwith thunderstorm potential most likely added in the coming shifts, \nbut winds may be light and variable should the circulation remain \nweak. This situation will continue to be refined as confidence \nincreases naturally with time. A return to drier weather is possible \nin the latter half of the week with isolated light showers (10% \nchance) included.",
